#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100779 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076adf41-55fa-5e02-a5f4-92e1a61c4181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y-7{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447253} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebf0f82-e343-52b7-8a49-05d015118207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y+7{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447263} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb0db6-5ea4-55e4-8c9d-e19190d4f9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35-7{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447264} {:answer/id #uuid "c5230433-2add-5000-ad4e-95ef2322db26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447279} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b4484f-b8cf-59f6-8eb0-156f59685802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "076adf41-55fa-5e02-a5f4-92e1a61c4181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y-7{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447253} :answer-field/id #uuid "05ec70e4-e3ba-524d-be45-20e5c451f94b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47725b1d-6489-53a4-9d3a-6bd1c0f75d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-119267" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=35y-7{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=7y(5-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074ac593-7a3a-5f93-b735-2ee808d813ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250612} {:answer/id #uuid "26237e43-f926-52c0-b38b-101da366fa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250882} {:answer/id #uuid "541bd8b2-71a6-5943-ad57-14b07db292c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :an