b/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=3-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447265} {:answer/id #uuid "899816c6-722e-5949-9ce4-1a89698b454a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447269} {:answer/id #uuid "9d888226-313c-58ec-b603-27ecf200a67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447271} {:answer/id #uuid "e86aa13e-6c97-5b82-b6ff-679f462956c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447285} {:answer/id #uuid "fff1f2be-796e-56e8-9751-6260916e4506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=9-{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "899816c6-722e-5949-9ce4-1a89698b454a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447269} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6064b7d-2ce9-5405-a285-35f32d2b4760" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "337615da-7bae-5da8-aef5-ed48be968edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83852" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3y(\\frac{1}{3}-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2fabba3-d0eb-5536-9a95-97abc2710798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-2.5P(2\\,000-P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447273} {:answer/id #uuid "a406afc4-d960-5034-b51e-8c31d270b58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,000P(2.5+P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447274} {:answer/id #uuid "b5266803-3e57-51fe-9003-3e15528da116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2\\,000P(2.5-P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447275} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f243f9-3128-53cf-8e9c-a561972a655a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000+P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447276} {:answer/id #uuid "df46b27d-b130-5ffa-8f32-a6057c1940a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000-P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df46b27d-b130-5ffa-8f32-a6057c1940a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000-P)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447284} :answer-field/id #uuid "611d3f14-6e92-5707-9d1c-74f01cafe308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cfc9e704-11ab-5799-a5b1-c0cc751bf228" :question/math-academy-id "q-83855" :question/problem "A rumor is spreading in a school of $2\\,000$ students. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who have heard the rumor is jointly proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the number who have not heard the rumor. If the constant of proportionality is $2.5,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the rumor's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have heard the rumor at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of people who have heard the rumor follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who heard the rumor is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2.5P(2\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ecad4cf-6f4e-5dbe-b398-a7011470a8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348575} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3c528d-02f7-5617-bad3-395c086edfd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349001} {:answer/id #uuid "53f761e0-2657-544a-ae8e-012f164d09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349213} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8314de-e026-5b53-84ac-59d41ea31d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349306} {:answer/id #uuid "821c67f9-d1de-5e15-a4af-e7f59909a2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f761e0-2657-544a-ae8e-012f164d09f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349213} :answer-field/id #uuid "e052f5ca-7248-5e20-b2b0-06564e0b1fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07dd1e2f-c2b7-5df5-89ff-72a9c31796dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83857" :question/problem "The growth rate of the seal population $P(t)$ on a certain South African island is jointly proportional to the seal population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the island's carrying capacity and the seal population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,002$ and the maximum possible seal population is $50\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the seal population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the seal population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the seal population, the seal population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the seal population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25935ef1-27a2-5178-a4d2-d936bbae9e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,1P(10\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447258} {:answer/id #uuid "36f03a9c-6c86-514e-8536-0c6bba5a3abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,1P(10\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447262} {:answer/id #uuid "76b57d70-a086-5a76-ad08-1757bf1a92fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,1P(10\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447268} {:answer/id #uuid "94d0e760-c927-55f9-8532-b3496e6146ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=10\\,000P(0.000\\,1+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447270} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bd8bc3-8421-52dd-bc14-38e7879c5978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=10\\,000P(0.000\\,1-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b57d70-a086-5a76-ad08-1757bf1a92fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,1P(10\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447268} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8afcb64-7f3e-534f-a7ec-9869fa2663b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e56756c2-a487-52f8-beba-b87d8eaed329" :question/math-academy-id "q-83862" :question/problem "The growth rate of the woodpecker population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the woodpecker population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the woodpecker population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,1$ and the maximum possible woodpecker population is $10\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the woodpecker population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the woodpecker population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the woodpecker population, the woodpecker population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the woodpecker population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,1P(10\\,000-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a10537-d01c-588a-af50-1725a012a2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=60\\,000P(0.75+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447254} {:answer/id #uuid "2734d77f-afd6-5eac-babe-fd41a107ee1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.75P(60\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447260} {:answer/id #uuid "74b013fb-7bc8-5cdf-9d89-9d9560979c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.75P(60\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447267} {:answer/id #uuid "d881d9cc-7679-5e20-949e-5c6732e6c221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.75P(60\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447283} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f11950-bdd9-5f8f-89ec-bd8c9b4bc5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=60\\,000P(0.75-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d881d9cc-7679-5e20-949e-5c6732e6c221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.75P(60\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447283} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a96f53c-28f1-5a63-8cc1-08c4c076a8a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6009a1c0-18f1-5130-b32c-9c18394dafe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-85975" :question/problem "Some viral content on the internet starts spreading through an audience of $60\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ who have seen the content is jointly proportional to the number of people who have seen the content at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the number who have not. If the constant of proportionality is $0.75,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of people who have seen the content is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have seen the content at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of people who have seen the content follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.75,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=60\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have seen the content is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.75P(60\\,000-P).\n$$"}]}
